## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates mastery of several advanced graph techniques: DAG dynamic programming, multi-criteria optimization, path reconstruction, and cycle handling. It tests the ability to reason about edge attributes with different behaviors (skip-able vs mandatory latency) and implement lexicographic optimization where hops are prioritized over latency.

## Key Concepts
- Topological sorting for efficient DAG processing
- Lexicographic multi-objective optimization (hops first, latency second)
- Predecessor-based path reconstruction
- Cycle detection using DFS and graph condensation
- Edge attribute handling with conditional weight computation

## Code Example
```javascript
function shortestPathDAG(graph, s, t) {
  // Assumes graph is a DAG. Each edge has: latency w, skip boolean
  const order = topologicalSort(graph);
  const best = new Map();
  const pred = new Map();

  best.set(s, [0, 0]); // [hops, latency]

  for (const u of order) {
    if (!best.has(u)) continue;
    const [hops, latency] = best.get(u);

    for (const {to: v, latency: w, skip} of graph.edgesFrom(u)) {
      const cand = [hops + 1, latency + (skip ? 0 : w)];
      const current = best.get(v);
      if (!current || lexicographicallySmaller(cand, current)) {
        best.set(v, cand);
        pred.set(v, u);
      }
    }
  }

  return reconstructPath(pred, s, t);
}

function detectCycles(graph) {
  const visited = new Set();
  const recStack = new Set();

  function dfs(u) {
    if (recStack.has(u)) return true; // Cycle detected
    if (visited.has(u)) return false;

    visited.add(u);
    recStack.add(u);

    for (const {to: v} of graph.edgesFrom(u)) {
      if (dfs(v)) return true;
    }

    recStack.delete(u);
    return false;
  }

  return Array.from(graph.nodes()).some(dfs);
}
```

## Adaptation for Cyclic Graphs
If cycles are present, first detect them using DFS with a recursion stack. For graphs with cycles, apply strongly connected components (SCC) decomposition to create a condensation DAG, then solve on the condensed graph. Alternatively, use Dijkstra's algorithm modified for lexicographic optimization where the edge weight is the tuple (1, w or 0 based on skip).

## Why This Question Is Asked
This question tests dynamic programming intuition on a constrained path counting problem with simple transitions and boundary conditions.

## Key Concepts
- **Recurrence relation:** f(i) = f(i+1) + f(i+2) with modulo arithmetic
- **Memoization vs. tabulation:** Trade-offs between recursive top-down with caching versus iterative bottom-up approaches
- **Edge conditions:** Blocked start/end positions, single-cell arrays

## Code Example
```javascript
function countPaths(blocked) {
  const n = blocked.length;
  const MOD = 1000000007;
  if (blocked[0]) return 0;

  // Memoized DFS
  const memo = Array(n).fill(-1);
  function dfs(i) {
    if (i === n - 1) return blocked[i] ? 0 : 1;
    if (i >= n || blocked[i]) return 0;
    if (memo[i] !== -1) return memo[i];
    memo[i] = (dfs(i + 1) + dfs(i + 2)) % MOD;
    return memo[i];
  }
  return dfs(0);
}

// Bottom-up DP (O(1) space)
function countPathsDP(blocked) {
  const n = blocked.length;
  const MOD = 1000000007;
  if (blocked[0]) return 0;

  let next2 = 1, next1 = blocked[n - 1] ? 0 : 1;
  for (let i = n - 2; i >= 0; i--) {
    const curr = blocked[i] ? 0 : (next1 + next2) % MOD;
    next2 = next1;
    next1 = curr;
  }
  return next1;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests your understanding of counting DP, covering both memoization (top-down) and tabulation (bottom-up) approaches, along with space optimization and edge-case handling.

## Key Concepts

- Subset sum counting with each element used at most once
- Memoization (top-down) with recursive DFS and memo table
- Tabulation (bottom-up) with 1D space optimization to O(S)
- Modulo arithmetic to prevent integer overflow

## Edge Cases

- **Empty subset (N=0)**: If N=0 and S=0, return 1 (one valid subset - empty set). If S>0, return 0.
- **Target S=0**: Always return 1 (the empty subset counts as one valid subset).
- **All elements greater than S**: No valid subsets exist, return 0.

## Code Example

```javascript
function countSubsets(nums, target) {
  const mod = 1_000_000_007;
  const n = nums.length;

  // Top-down with memoization
  const memo = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(target + 1).fill(-1));
  function dfs(i, s) {
    if (s === 0) return 1;
    if (i === 0) return 0;
    if (memo[i][s] !== -1) return memo[i][s];
    memo[i][s] = dfs(i - 1, s) % mod;
    if (s >= nums[i - 1]) {
      memo[i][s] = (memo[i][s] + dfs(i - 1, s - nums[i - 1])) % mod;
    }
    return memo[i][s];
  }
  return dfs(n, target);
}

// Bottom-up with space optimization
function countSubsetsBU(nums, target) {
  const mod = 1_000_000_007;
  const dp = Array(target + 1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;

  for (const num of nums) {
    for (let s = target; s >= num; s--) {
      dp[s] = (dp[s] + dp[s - num]) % mod;
    }
  }
  return dp[target];
}
```

Both solutions run in O(N×S) time with the bottom-up approach using O(S) space.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of DAG longest-path DP and cycle detection using topological sort, a common scheduling/build scenario.

## Key Concepts
- Topological sort (Kahn's algorithm)
- Longest path in a DAG via DP
- Cycle detection via processed-node count
- Handling multiple prerequisites and parallel tasks

## Code Example
```javascript
function computeEarliestFinish(n, durations, edges) {
  const adj = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(n).fill(0);
  for (const [u, v] of edges) { adj[u].push(v); indeg[v]++; }
  const est = durations.slice();
  const q = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) if (indeg[i] === 0) q.push(i);
  let processed = 0;
  while (q.length) {
    const u = q.shift();
    processed++;
    for (const v of adj[u]) {
      if (est[v] < est[u] + durations[v]) est[v] = est[u] + durations[v];
      if (--indeg[v] === 0) q.push(v);
    }
  }
  if (processed !== n) throw new Error('Cycle detected');
  return { est, total: Math.max(...est) };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reconstruct the actual critical path from est and predecessors?
- How modify for weighted edges representing inter-task setup times instead of node durations?
- How to scale and detect cycles efficiently in streaming graphs?

Why This Is Asked
- Tests dynamic augmentation of a trie with aggregated metrics and tie-breaking logic.

Key Concepts
- Trie augmentation with subtreeSum and subtreeBest; O(length) updates; O(length) queries.
- Correct handling of weight deltas, insertions, and removals; lexicographic tiebreaks.

Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Map();
    this.subtreeSum = 0;
    this.bestWord = null;
    this.bestWeight = 0;
  }
}
class DynamicDict {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
    this.wordWeights = new Map();
  }
  add(word, delta) {
    let node = this.root;
    const newWeight = (this.wordWeights.get(word) || 0) + delta;
    this.wordWeights.set(word, newWeight);
    for (const char of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) {
        node.children.set(char, new TrieNode());
      }
      node = node.children.get(char);
      node.subtreeSum += delta;
      if (newWeight > node.bestWeight || 
          (newWeight === node.bestWeight && word < node.bestWord)) {
        node.bestWord = word;
        node.bestWeight = newWeight;
      }
    }
  }
  query(prefix) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of prefix) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) {
        return [0, null];
      }
      node = node.children.get(char);
    }
    return [node.subtreeSum, node.bestWord];
  }
}
```

Complexity Analysis
- `add(word, delta)`: O(|word|) time, O(1) extra space per character
- `query(prefix)`: O(|prefix|) time, O(1) extra space

Edge Cases
- Negative deltas potentially removing words completely
- Multiple words with equal maximum weights requiring lexicographic tie-breaking
- Empty queries and non-existent prefixes
- Words sharing common prefixes affecting aggregate calculations

## Why This Is Asked
This probes understanding of iterative approaches, partition logic, and complexity trade-offs between quicksort and mergesort. 

## Key Concepts
- Iterative quicksort with explicit stack
- Hoare partition scheme and pivot strategy
- Time/space complexity comparisons with mergesort

## Code Example
```javascript
function iterativeQuicksort(a){
  const n=a.length;
  const stack=[[0,n-1]];
  const swap=(i,j)=>{let t=a[i]; a[i]=a[j]; a[j]=t;};
  while(stack.length){
    const [l,r]=stack.pop();
    if(l>=r) continue;
    let pivot=a[(l+r)>>1];
    let i=l, j=r;
    while(i<=j){
      while(a[i]<pivot) i++;
      while(a[j]>pivot) j--;
      if(i<=j){ swap(i,j); i++; j--; }
    }
    if(l<j) stack.push([l,j]);
    if(i<r) stack.push([i,r]);
  }
  return a;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you modify to guarantee O(n log n) worst-case?
- Compare stability with mergesort; when would you choose one over the other?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses interval DP, proper subproblem decomposition, and practical optimization with prefix sums. Builds intuition for product costs over contiguous blocks.

## Key Concepts
- Interval DP on [i, j]
- Prefix sums for O(1) range sums
- Bottom-up filling by increasing length; base case 0 for single element

## Code Example
```javascript
function minMergeCost(stones){
  const n = stones.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) pref[i+1] = pref[i] + stones[i];
  const sum = (i,j) => pref[j+1]-pref[i];
  const dp = Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let len=2; len<=n; len++){
    for(let i=0; i+len-1<n; i++){
      const j = i+len-1;
      let best = Infinity;
      for(let k=i; k<j; k++){
        const cost = dp[i][k] + dp[k+1][j] + sum(i,j);
        if(cost < best) best = cost;
      }
      dp[i][j] = best;
    }
  }
  return n>0 ? dp[0][n-1] : 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for K-ary merging (K > 2)?
- What optimizations apply if n is large and many merges share sums?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests knowledge of two classic sorts, practical optimization, and memory trade-offs. A hybrid approach reveals ability to tune algorithms for real data patterns.

## Key Concepts
- Hybrid sorting threshold and when it helps
- Stability via merge vs insertion behavior
- Space usage with a single auxiliary buffer

## Code Example
```javascript
function sortBatch(arr) {
  const n = arr.length;
  const buf = new Array(n);
  function insertionSort(a, left, right) {
    for (let i = left + 1; i <= right; i++) {
      const key = a[i];
      let j = i - 1;
      while (j >= left && a[j] > key) {
        a[j + 1] = a[j];
        j--;
      }
      a[j + 1] = key;
    }
  }
  function merge(left, mid, right) {
    let i = left, j = mid + 1, k = left;
    while (i <= mid && j <= right) {
      if (arr[i] <= arr[j]) buf[k++] = arr[i++];
      else buf[k++] = arr[j++];
    }
    while (i <= mid) buf[k++] = arr[i++];
    while (j <= right) buf[k++] = arr[j++];
    for (let t = left; t <= right; t++) arr[t] = buf[t];
  }
  function sort(l, r) {
    if (l >= r) return;
    if (r - l + 1 <= 16) {
      insertionSort(arr, l, r);
    } else {
      const m = Math.floor((l + r) / 2);
      sort(l, m);
      sort(m + 1, r);
      merge(l, m, r);
    }
  }
  sort(0, n - 1);
}
```

## Why This Question Is Asked
This problem evaluates the ability to extend classic DP patterns (like the staircase problem) with an additional resource dimension, while also testing space optimization skills and recursive thinking via memoization. It distinguishes candidates who understand both bottom-up and top-down approaches, and who reason through edge cases systematically.

## Key Concepts
- DP with an extra budget constraint (two-dimensional state: position + accumulated cost)
- Space optimization using rolling arrays to reduce O(n·B) space to O(B)
- Memoization on composite state (i, b) for the recursive variant
- Correct base case handling when starting from a virtual position before index 0

## Code Example
```javascript
const MOD = 1_000_000_007n;

function countWays(cost, B) {
  const n = cost.length;
  if (n === 0) return 0;

  // bottom-up with rolling arrays
  let prev2 = new Array(B + 1).fill(0);
  let prev1 = new Array(B + 1).fill(0);

  // base: step 0
  if (cost[0] <= B) prev2[cost[0]] = 1;

  // base: step 1
  if (n > 1 && cost[1] <= B) prev1[cost[1]] = 1;

  // step 2 onwards
  for (let i = 2; i < n; i++) {
    const cur = new Array(B + 1).fill(0);
    for (let b = cost[i]; b <= B; b++) {
      cur[b] = (prev1[b - cost[i]] + prev2[b - cost[i]]) % MOD;
    }
    prev2 = prev1;
    prev1 = cur;
  }

  return n === 1 ? prev2[B] : prev1[B];
}

// memoized DFS variant
function countWaysDFS(cost, B) {
  const n = cost.length;
  const memo = new Map();

  function dfs(i, b) {
    if (b < 0) return 0;
    if (i < 0) return b === 0 ? 1 : 0;
    const key = `${i},${b}`;
    if (memo.has(key)) return memo.get(key);
    const result = (dfs(i - 1, b - cost[i]) + dfs(i - 2, b - cost[i])) % MOD;
    memo.set(key, result);
    return result;
  }

  return dfs(n - 1, B);
}
```

