## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of prefix-based searching using a practical, lightweight data structure. It combines Trie fundamentals with per-node augmentation to enable fast prefix queries in real time—useful in autocomplete systems, IP routing, and spell checking.

## Key Concepts
- **Trie structure**: Each node contains 26 children (one per letter) and maintains a pass counter representing how many words pass through that node
- **insert operation**: O(m) time, where m is word length; creates nodes as needed and increments pass at each node visited
- **count_with_prefix operation**: O(k) time, where k is prefix length; traverses to the prefix node and returns its pass counter
- **Handling duplicates**: Pass counters naturally accumulate, so duplicate insertions simply increment the counter without special handling

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Array(26).fill(null);
    this.pass = 0;
    this.isWord = false;
  }
}

class Trie {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
  }

  insert(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of word) {
      const idx = char.charCodeAt(0) - 97;
      if (!node.next[idx]) node.next[idx] = new TrieNode();
      node.pass++;
      node = node.next[idx];
    }
    node.pass++;
    node.isWord = true;
  }

  countWithPrefix(prefix) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of prefix) {
      const idx = char.charCodeAt(0) - 97;
      if (!node.next[idx]) return 0;
      node = node.next[idx];
    }
    return node.pass;
  }
}

// Example usage
const trie = new Trie();
['cat', 'car', 'cart', 'dog', 'dot'].forEach(w => trie.insert(w));
console.log(trie.countWithPrefix('ca')); // 3
console.log(trie.countWithPrefix('car')); // 1
console.log(trie.countWithPrefix('d')); // 2
console.log(trie.countWithPrefix('do')); // 2
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design streaming state with sliding windows, using hash maps, per-id deques (linked lists), and a heap for ranking. Emphasizes correctness under out-of-order arrivals and lazy expirations.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding-window maintenance per key using a deque
- Lazy expiration and lazy heap invalidation
- Top-K extraction via a max-heap with versioning

## Code Example
```javascript
class RealTimeTopKWindow {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.byId = new Map(); // id -> { deque: [], sum, version }
    this.globalMax = new MinHeap((a,b)=>b.sum-a.sum); // max-heap emulation
  }
  addEvent(id, value, ts) {
    // Ensure id exists
    const rec = this.byId.get(id) || { deque: [], sum: 0, version: 0 };
    // expire old
    while (rec.deque.length && rec.deque[0].ts <= ts - this.W) {
      const old = rec.deque.shift();
      rec.sum -= old.value;
    }
    rec.deque.push({ ts, value });
    rec.sum += value;
    rec.version++;
    this.byId.set(id, rec);
    this.globalMax.push({ sum: rec.sum, id, ver: rec.version });
  }
  getTopK(K) {
    const res = [];
    const seen = new Set();
    while (res.length < K) {
      const top = this.globalMax.pop();
      if (!top) break;
      const cur = this.byId.get(top.id);
      if (!cur || cur.version !== top.ver || top.sum !== cur.sum) continue; // stale
      if (!seen.has(top.id)) {
        res.push(top.id);
        seen.add(top.id);
      }
    }
    return res;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt if W varies by id? 
- How to scale to millions of ids with bounded memory?
